# backend-patterns (Cursor)

## Layering

- **Repository pattern**: abstract data access behind an interface
  (`findAll`, `findById`, `create`, `update`, `delete`) so the storage
  engine can be swapped without touching business logic.
- **Service layer**: business logic lives here, calling the repository —
  keep it separate from HTTP concerns.
- **Middleware**: request/response pipeline (auth, logging, rate limiting)
  as composable wrappers around handlers.

## Database

- Select only needed columns, never `select('*')` in hot paths.
- Avoid N+1: batch-fetch related rows once (collect ids, one query, map
  back) instead of querying per item in a loop.
- Wrap multi-step writes in a transaction (DB-native or an RPC/stored
  procedure) so partial failure rolls back cleanly.

## Caching

Cache-aside pattern: check cache first, on miss fetch from DB and populate
cache with a TTL, invalidate explicitly on writes. Keep cache logic in a
dedicated layer (e.g. a `CachedRepository` wrapper) rather than scattering
`redis.get`/`set` calls through business logic.

## Error handling

Centralize error handling: a typed `ApiError` class with a status code,
caught once at the boundary and mapped to a consistent JSON error shape.
Never leak stack traces to the client — log the full error server-side,
return a generic message to the caller. Retry transient failures with
exponential backoff, not a tight loop.

## Auth

Verify JWTs server-side on every protected route; never trust a
client-supplied role/user id. Layer role/permission checks as a
higher-order wrapper around the handler so authorization is explicit and
reusable, not duplicated per route.

## Rate limiting

Must use a shared store (Redis, gateway, or the platform's native
limiter) — never a per-process in-memory counter for production APIs, since
those reset on deploy and don't work across replicas or serverless
instances. See `api-design` for the HTTP contract and `security-review`
for abuse-case review.

## Background jobs

Queue work instead of blocking the request — even a simple in-process
queue beats doing slow work synchronously in a handler. For anything that
must survive a process restart, use a real queue (not an in-memory array).

## Logging

Structured JSON logs (timestamp, level, message, context) over bare
`console.log` strings — makes logs greppable and parseable by log
aggregators. Include a request id in every log line tied to one request.
